reduceSpace -- reduce the generators of a space
***********************************************

  * Usage: 
        S = reduceSpace T
  * Inputs:
      * T, an instance of the type *note DualSpace: DualSpace,, or an instance
        of the type *note PolySpace: PolySpace,
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Monomials => ..., default value null
      * *note Tolerance: sortSolutions_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmTolerance=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value .000001, the tolerance of a numerical computation
  * Outputs:
      * S, an instance of the type *note DualSpace: DualSpace,, or an instance
        of the type *note PolySpace: PolySpace,

Description
===========

Reduces the generators of a DualSpace or PolySpace so that the new generators
are linearly independent, and each has a distinct lead monomial.  This is
achieved by Gaussian reduction.

+------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = CC[x,y];                         |
+------------------------------------------+
|i2 : T = polySpace matrix{{x,y,x-y+1e-10}}|
|                                          |
|o2 = | x y x-y+1e-10 |                    |
|                                          |
|o2 : PolySpace                            |
+------------------------------------------+
|i3 : S = reduceSpace T                    |
|                                          |
|o3 = | x y |                              |
|                                          |
|o3 : PolySpace                            |
+------------------------------------------+
|i4 : S = reduceSpace(T, Tolerance=>1e-12) |
|                                          |
|o4 = | x y 1 |                            |
|                                          |
|o4 : PolySpace                            |
+------------------------------------------+

Ways to use reduceSpace:
========================

  * "reduceSpace(DualSpace)"
  * "reduceSpace(PolySpace)"

For the programmer
==================

